WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.867
Some equations have variable coefficients and their solutions cannot be written with elementary functions.

2
00:00:08.867 --> 00:00:13.468
For example Airy’s equation, y double prime equals x y.

3
00:00:13.468 --> 00:00:23.004
In such cases we look for the solution as an infinite power series and compute the coefficients one after another.

4
00:00:23.004 --> 00:00:25.430
This is also easy to program.

5
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.924
Write the equation as y double prime plus p y prime plus q y equals zero.

6
00:00:31.924 --> 00:00:38.253
If p and q are analytic at the point x zero, the point is called ordinary.

7
00:00:38.253 --> 00:00:49.286
Then every solution expands in a series; the first two coefficients are arbitrary and the rest follow from a recurrence relation.

8
00:00:49.536 --> 00:00:51.326
Take Airy’s equation.

9
00:00:51.326 --> 00:01:04.278
Differentiate the series twice and shift the index by two: y double prime is the sum of n plus two times n plus one times a n plus two times x to the n.

10
00:01:04.278 --> 00:01:12.374
On the right is x y, the sum of a n minus one times x to the n, which starts at n equal to one.

11
00:01:12.624 --> 00:01:19.839
Match the coefficients of x to the zero: two a two equals zero, so a two is zero.

12
00:01:19.839 --> 00:01:30.083
For n at least one we get the recurrence: a n plus two equals a n minus one divided by n plus two times n plus one.

13
00:01:30.083 --> 00:01:34.982
The step is three: coefficients are linked three apart.

14
00:01:35.232 --> 00:01:42.291
Compute: a three is a zero over six, a six is a zero over one hundred eighty.

15
00:01:42.291 --> 00:01:48.982
a four is a one over twelve, and a seven is a one over five hundred four.

16
00:01:48.982 --> 00:01:55.766
The two series form a fundamental set and converge on the whole real line.

17
00:01:56.016 --> 00:02:03.193
The graph shows a partial sum of the solution with a zero equal to one and a one equal to zero,

18
00:02:03.193 --> 00:02:04.930
up to x to the twelfth.

19
00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.876
For negative x the solution oscillates,

20
00:02:07.876 --> 00:02:14.222
because there the equation looks like y double prime plus a positive number times y.

21
00:02:14.222 --> 00:02:16.639
For positive x it grows quickly.

22
00:02:16.639 --> 00:02:18.830
One equation, two behaviours.

23
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:27.602
Question: what is the recurrence for y double prime minus x y prime minus y equals zero?

24
00:02:27.602 --> 00:02:33.605
Substitute the series and write the coefficient of x to the n.

25
00:02:33.605 --> 00:02:37.382
Pause the video and choose your answer.
